  "provider_query": "English Wikipedia HTTP article: the default TCP port",
  "answer": "The English Wikipedia **HTTP** article states that unencrypted HTTP typically uses **TCP port 80**.\n\n1. **HTTP – Wikipedia** — In HTTP implementations, TCP/IP connections use well-known ports, typically port 80 if unencrypted (port 443 if encrypted). An example request–response is for a server on port 80.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP\n\n2. **Port (computer networking) – Wikipedia** — By default, HTTP uses port 80 and HTTPS uses port 443. Port 80 is listed for Hypertext Transfer Protocol.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_(computer_networking)\n\n3. **List of TCP and UDP port numbers – Wikipedia** — Port 80 is assigned to Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP); versions 1.x and 2 use TCP (HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP).  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_TCP_and_UDP_port_numbers\n\n4. **URL – Wikipedia** — A browser usually dereferences an HTTP URL by requesting the host, by default on port 80.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/URL\n\n5. **Port 80 – Wikipedia** (disambiguation) — TCP port 80 is most often used by Hypertext Transfer Protocol.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_80\n\n6. **Registered port – Wikipedia** — Ports 0–1023 are system/well-known ports (the range that includes HTTP’s port 80).  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Registered_port\n\n7. **Open port – Wikipedia** — An open port is a TCP or UDP port configured to accept packets; services such as web pages need their ports open to be reachable.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_port\n\n8. **Help:URL – Wikipedia** — English Wikipedia URLs use `https://` (scheme-level addressing; HTTP’s default TCP port remains 80 when the `http` scheme is used without an explicit port).  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:URL",
